//
// A METHOD CALL on a nullable collection names the `?`, not a method that plainly exists.
//
// This is the sharper of the two, because of WHERE it lands: the method dispatch matched no
// `τ?` arm, so the STRUCT-FIELD fallback took over and named the CONTENT def — *"Unknown field
// vector.remove"*.  The reader is told a method they can read in STDLIB.md does not exist, and
// sent to look for a misspelling, while the fault is one character wide and on the receiver.
//
// The refusal itself is right — `(N-Coal)` gives a `τ?` no implicit unwrap — so this is
// loft#1453's cure (`for` / `map` / `filter`) carried out of its one home to a site it did
// not reach.  The sibling spelling `rev(c)` is `1450b`, and they are separate files because the
// `rev` refusal leaves the PARSE desynchronised, so a later expectation never gets its chance —
// not because the harness stops at one (it checks every annotation and fails on each miss).
//
// ⚠ The METHOD this asks about changed with `(N-Chain-Place)`, and the change is the rule, not
// a weakening of this guard.  It used to ask `.remove`, which is a MUTATING method: an
// assignment target and a mutating receiver are PLACES, so they take no discharge and do
// nothing when the receiver is absent (owner ruling 2026-09-08).  Asking a place for a
// discharge is the one thing this refusal must NOT do — the cure it would advertise, `?`,
// builds an empty collection and removes from that, which loses the write in silence.  So the
// subject moves to a READING method, where the refusal is right and still exactly as written:
// `.sum` on a `vector<integer>?` names the `?` instead of *"Unknown field vector.sum"*.
// The place half is guarded in `1450d`, including the `.remove` / `.clear` cells this file
// used to own.
//
// Axes it HOLDS FIXED:  the receiver is DECLARED `vector<…>?`, so the refusal is reachable
//                       today without loft#1450's `(N-Prop)` leg.
//
// @falsified-at: 8ba3e755 — reports *"Unknown field vector.sum"* there, so the
//   `@EXPECT_ERROR` misses and the file fails.

struct Bag1450c { xs: vector<integer>? }

// @EXPECT_ERROR: cannot call `.sum` on vector<integer>? because it is NULLABLE
fn sum_it(b: Bag1450c) {
  q = b.xs.sum();
}
